  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Nested and Repeating Square-Root Expressions",
    "description": "Recognize structure in nested radicals and derive a valid value before calculating.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach the chapter's nested and repeating radical patterns, including expressions such as √(a+√(a+...)) when a finite or infinite pattern is explicitly specified. For an infinite repeating expression, define its value as x only when the expression is convergent and non-negative, derive the resulting equation from the repeated structure, solve it, and select the root consistent with the original expression. Distinguish an infinite nested radical from a finite sum and verify by substitution or partial approximations. Also cover finite nested roots and simple radical identities from the source. Learners should expose the repeated structure, justify the admissible solution, and avoid guessing from a few terms."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Nested radicals are evaluated from the inside out. A repeating infinite radical can sometimes be represented by a fixed-point equation, but only after respecting convergence and the non-negative principal-root condition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a finite nested expression, evaluate the innermost root first. √(9+√16)=√(9+4)=√13. The outer radical does not distribute over addition; the inside root must be resolved before simplifying the outer radicand.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
